WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:08.992
In his chambers at Gloucester castle, the earl said to Edmund, "I do not like taking orders from Lear's daughters and their husbands.

2
00:00:08.993 --> 00:00:14.111
But they are our rulers now. They tell me I must not help the king."

3
00:00:14.112 --> 00:00:18.206
"How cruel!" exclaimed Edmund, pretending to be shocked.

4
00:00:18.207 --> 00:00:23.492
"But there is more. I have a letter from Cordelia, now the queen of France.

5
00:00:23.493 --> 00:00:29.388
She has learned of her father's troubles, and the French army is on its way to defend the king.

6
00:00:29.389 --> 00:00:35.260
Now I shall go look for him. If Cornwall asks for me, tell him I have gone to bed.

7
00:00:35.261 --> 00:00:40.506
Do not say a word to him of these matters." And Gloucester went out into the storm.

8
00:00:40.507 --> 00:00:47.462
Edmund could not believe his luck. "I shall tell Cornwall everything immediately," he said to himself.

9
00:00:47.463 --> 00:00:53.415
"When he hears that my father has disobeyed him, Cornwall will surely reward me."

10
00:00:53.416 --> 00:00:58.843
Meanwhile, Lear was wandering on the heath, accompanied by the faithful Kent.

11
00:00:58.844 --> 00:01:07.481
"Blow winds, blow!" roared Lear. "Lightning, burn my white head! You will still be kinder than my daughters!"

12
00:01:07.482 --> 00:01:12.394
A few days earlier, Kent had also received a letter from Cordelia.

13
00:01:12.395 --> 00:01:17.892
"I am coming to England with an army," she wrote. "We shall land at Dover."

14
00:01:17.893 --> 00:01:22.554
"If we can get the king to Dover," Kent thought, "he might be safe."

15
00:01:22.555 --> 00:01:26.586
"My lord," he said to Lear, "we must get away from here."

16
00:01:26.587 --> 00:01:35.475
But Lear continued to rage. "The gods are punishing the wicked with this storm. But I am a man more sinned against than sinning."

17
00:01:35.483 --> 00:01:40.947
"I see an old shack in the distance," said Kent. "It will give you some shelter."

18
00:01:40.948 --> 00:01:46.613
Lear stared at Kent. "Good Caius, I fear I am losing my mind.

19
00:01:46.614 --> 00:01:51.618
This storm seems horrible to you, but to me, it seems like nothing.

20
00:01:51.619 --> 00:01:56.697
When one experiences greater suffering, one barely notices the lesser."

21
00:01:56.698 --> 00:02:01.165
When they got to the shack, Lear looked inside and then jumped back.

22
00:02:01.166 --> 00:02:08.120
"What is that strange creature?" he said fearfully. Kent peered in and saw a man curled up in the corner.

23
00:02:08.121 --> 00:02:14.588
He had mud on his face, and his hair was dirty and tangled. He was muttering nonsense.

24
00:02:14.589 --> 00:02:16.953
"Who are you?" said Kent.

25
00:02:16.954 --> 00:02:21.932
The man stood up. "I am Poor Tom," he cackled.

26
00:02:21.933 --> 00:02:31.154
"Wicked spirits have chased me through fire and flame and put poison in my porridge. I beg of you some charity." He held out his hand.

27
00:02:31.155 --> 00:02:36.511
"He's just a harmless madman and a beggar," said Kent to Lear.

28
00:02:36.512 --> 00:02:42.280
Neither man realized that Poor Tom was actually Edgar, Gloucester's son.

29
00:02:42.281 --> 00:02:47.574
Knowing his father wanted him dead, he was hiding in the countryside in disguise.

30
00:02:47.575 --> 00:02:52.296
"Did his cruel daughters make him crazy?" Lear ranted.

31
00:02:52.297 --> 00:02:56.698
"Why, he has only a thin blanket to protect him from the storm.

32
00:02:56.699 --> 00:03:04.841
Is man no more than this: a poor, bare, pitiful animal? Then I too am just like this beggar!"

33
00:03:04.842 --> 00:03:10.174
Lear began to tear off his own clothes as Kent tried to calm him.

34
00:03:10.175 --> 00:03:19.190
"Beware of the evil spirit! Obey your parents! Poor Tom is cold. Look!" cried Edgar, "here comes a walking fire."

35
00:03:19.191 --> 00:03:25.500
Gloucester appeared, carrying a torch. He did not recognize Edgar, his own son, either.

36
00:03:25.501 --> 00:03:31.683
"My lord," the earl said to Lear, "your daughters and the Duke of Cornwall have forbidden me to help you.

37
00:03:31.684 --> 00:03:37.512
But I cannot obey such heartless orders. Let me take you to a warmer shelter."

38
00:03:37.513 --> 00:03:43.317
"I must speak more with Poor Tom," muttered Lear. "He is very wise."

39
00:03:43.318 --> 00:03:49.031
"I fear his grief has made him lose his mind," whispered Kent to Gloucester.

40
00:03:49.032 --> 00:03:52.737
"I understand his grief too well," replied the earl.

41
00:03:52.738 --> 00:03:59.594
"I have a son I loved dearly, who also has betrayed me." He turned to Lear. "Come, my lord."

42
00:03:59.595 --> 00:04:07.317
Gloucester led Lear, Kent, and Edgar to a farmhouse. Then he left to find some food for the king.

43
00:04:07.318 --> 00:04:12.015
By now, Lear was acting as crazy as Edgar pretended to be.

44
00:04:12.016 --> 00:04:17.459
"Look," he babbled, pointing at two stools, "it's Goneril and Regan!

45
00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:27.769
We will put them on trial, and Poor Tom will be the judge! But first, I must rest." Exhausted, he lay down and fell asleep.

46
00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:34.301
Gloucester soon returned, very upset. "I just heard about a plot to kill the king," he told Kent.

47
00:04:34.302 --> 00:04:43.089
"You must take him to Dover, where he will be safe. Quick—I have a stretcher waiting outside. Lay the king on it and go now!"

48
00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:50.351
Back at Gloucester Castle, Edmund approached Cornwall and told him about the letter his father had received from Cordelia.

49
00:04:50.352 --> 00:04:58.732
"My lord," said Edmund, "it grieves me that my own father is a traitor. He supports Lear and Cordelia instead of you."

50
00:04:58.733 --> 00:05:09.126
Cornwall's eyes narrowed. "You have done well. I appoint you Earl of Gloucester in place of your father. He must be caught and punished."

51
00:05:09.127 --> 00:05:12.380
Cornwall then summoned his wife and Goneril.

52
00:05:12.381 --> 00:05:16.943
To Goneril, he said, "Spies say the French have landed.

53
00:05:16.944 --> 00:05:23.769
Ride to your castle and tell your husband to prepare for war. And seek out the traitor Gloucester."

54
00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:26.848
"He should hang," Regan declared.

55
00:05:26.849 --> 00:05:31.777
"Leave him to me," said Cornwall. "Edmund, accompany Goneril.

56
00:05:31.778 --> 00:05:36.340
You do not want to see what I shall do to your father when he is found."

57
00:05:36.341 --> 00:05:41.547
Shortly after Edmund and Goneril's departure, Gloucester returned to the castle.

58
00:05:41.548 --> 00:05:47.952
"Why, it's the traitor," Regan snarled. "Seize him," she ordered the servants.

59
00:05:47.953 --> 00:05:53.111
"What do you mean by this?" asked Gloucester, trembling. "You are my guests!"

60
00:05:53.112 --> 00:05:56.325
"Tie him to a chair," Cornwall commanded.

61
00:05:56.326 --> 00:05:59.717
"Where have you sent the king?" demanded Regan.

62
00:05:59.718 --> 00:06:06.741
"To Dover, to escape you and your sister," said Gloucester. "I will see the king in his rightful place again!"

63
00:06:06.742 --> 00:06:17.003
"You shall see nothing," hissed Cornwall. He leaned forward and gouged out Gloucester's right eye. Gloucester howled in pain.

64
00:06:17.004 --> 00:06:26.166
But when Cornwall moved to gouge out the left eye, a servant loyal to Gloucester shouted, "Stop, my lord!" He was waving a sword.

65
00:06:26.167 --> 00:06:31.213
"How dare you!" shouted Cornwall. He drew his sword, and they fought.

66
00:06:31.214 --> 00:06:40.142
The servant managed to strike Cornwall once, hard, before Regan grabbed a knife. Running up behind the servant, she stabbed him dead.

67
00:06:40.143 --> 00:06:47.745
"And now the other one!" Cornwall said, turning back to Gloucester. And he put out Gloucester's left eye.

68
00:06:47.746 --> 00:06:51.792
"Help! Where is Edmund?" groaned Gloucester.

69
00:06:51.793 --> 00:06:59.443
"You are calling for the wrong son," Regan told the old man. "It was Edmund who told us you betrayed us!"

70
00:06:59.444 --> 00:07:08.403
In a flash, Gloucester realized Edmund's plot. "Then I have done Edgar wrong," he sobbed. "May he forgive me!"

71
00:07:08.404 --> 00:07:13.911
"Throw him out," said Regan to the servants. "Let him smell his way to Dover."

72
00:07:13.912 --> 00:07:19.064
"Come, Regan, help me," moaned Cornwall. "I'm bleeding."

73
00:07:19.065 --> 00:07:23.659
Gloucester was thrown out of the castle and began to stumble down the road.

74
00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:28.097
He didn't get far before Edgar, still disguised, saw him.

75
00:07:28.097 --> 00:07:31.636
"My poor father," he thought, horrified.

76
00:07:31.636 --> 00:07:34.599
"Is someone there?" called out Gloucester.

77
00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:37.853
"It's Poor Tom," said Edgar.

78
00:07:37.854 --> 00:07:39.583
"The mad beggar?"

79
00:07:39.584 --> 00:07:42.417
"Yes, my lord. Bless you."

80
00:07:42.418 --> 00:07:48.060
"Do you know the road to Dover?" asked the earl. "I will pay you to take me there."

81
00:07:48.061 --> 00:07:52.640
"Here's my arm," said Edgar. "Poor Tom will lead you."

82
00:07:52.641 --> 00:08:02.669
Leaning on Edgar's arm, Gloucester said, "These are strange times, when madmen lead the blind." Together, they set off.

